\fs96 \cf2 Traits\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A trait is a set of requirements that a type must implement. You can think of it as a contract: a type that conforms to a trait guarantees that it implements all of the features of the trait.\
Traits are similar to Java interfaces, C++ concepts, Swift protocols, and Rust traits. If you're familiar with any of those features, Mojo traits solve the same basic problem.\
You've probably already seen some traits, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , used in example code. This section describes how traits work, how to use existing traits, and how to define your own traits.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Background\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In dynamically-typed languages like Python, you don't need to explicitly declare that two classes are similar. This is easiest to show by example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 \uc0\u55357 \u56333  Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 class\cf13  \cf16 Duck\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Quack."\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 class\cf13  \cf16 StealthCow\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Moo!"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  \cf16 make_it_quack\cf5 (\cf13 maybe_a_duck\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 try:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         maybe_a_duck\cf5 .\cf13 quack\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 except:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Not a duck."\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 make_it_quack\cf5 (\cf13 Duck\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15 make_it_quack\cf5 (\cf13 StealthCow\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Duck
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StealthCow
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  classes aren't related in any way, but they both define a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method, so they work the same in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 make_it_quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function. This works because Python uses dynamic dispatch\'97it identifies the methods to call at runtime. So 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 make_it_quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  doesn't care what types you're passing it, only the fact that they implement the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method.\
In a statically-typed environment, this approach doesn't work: Mojo functions require you to specify the type of each argument. If you wanted to write this example in Mojo without traits, you'd need to write a function overload for each input type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 Duck\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Quack"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 StealthCow\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Moo!"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  \cf16 make_it_quack\cf5 (\cf13 definitely_a_duck\cf5 :\cf13  Duck\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     definitely_a_duck\cf5 .\cf13 quack\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  \cf16 make_it_quack\cf5 (\cf13 not_a_duck\cf5 :\cf13  StealthCow\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     not_a_duck\cf5 .\cf13 quack\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 make_it_quack\cf5 (\cf13 Duck\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15 make_it_quack\cf5 (\cf13 StealthCow\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 Quack\cb1 \
\cb15 Moo!\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This isn't too bad with only two types. But the more types you want to support, the less practical this approach is.\
You might notice that the Mojo versions of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 make_it_quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  don't include the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 try/except
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement. We don't need it because Mojo's static type checking ensures that you can only pass instances of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Duck
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StealthCow
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  into the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 make_it_quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Using traits\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Traits solve this problem by letting you define a shared set of behaviors that types can implement. Then you can write a function that depends on the trait, rather than individual types. As an example, let's update the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 make_it_quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  example using traits. This will involve three steps:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Defining a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Quackable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Adding the trait to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Duck
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StealthCow
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  structs.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Updating the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 make_it_quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function to depend on the trait.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Defining a trait\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The first step is defining a trait that requires a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  Quackable\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A trait looks a lot like a struct, except it's introduced by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 trait
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword. Note that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method signature is followed by three dots (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ...
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). This indicates it isn't implemented within the trait. In this example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 quack
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a required method and must be implemented by any conforming struct.\
A trait can supply a default implementation, so conforming structs don't need to implement the method themselves. You can provide a full implementation or use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pass
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword. Using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pass
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  creates a no-op method that does nothing. In your conforming struct, you can choose to override this default implementation:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  Quackable\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 pass\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more information, see \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Default method implementations\cf2 \ulnone .\
A trait can also include 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members\'97compile-time constant values that must be defined by conforming structs. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members are useful for writing traits that describe generic types. For more information, see 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul comptime
\fs32 \cb1  members for generics\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Adding traits to structs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Next, we need some structs that conform to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Quackable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait. Since the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Duck
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StealthCow
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  structs above already implement the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method, all we need to do is add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Quackable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait to the traits it conforms to (in parenthesis, after the struct name).\
(If you're familiar with Python, this looks just like Python's inheritance syntax.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 Duck\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Quackable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Quack"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 StealthCow\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Quackable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Moo!"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The struct needs to implement any methods that are declared in the trait. The compiler enforces conformance: if a struct says it conforms to a trait, it must implement everything required by the trait, or the code won't compile.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Using a trait as a type bound\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Finally, you can define a function that takes a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Quackable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  make_it_quack\cf5 [\cf13 DuckType\cf5 :\cf13  Quackable\cf5 ](\cf13 maybe_a_duck\cf5 :\cf13  DuckType\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     maybe_a_duck\cf5 .\cf13 quack\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Or using the shorthand form:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  \cf16 make_it_quack2\cf5 (\cf13 maybe_a_duck\cf5 :\cf13  Some\cf5 [\cf13 Quackable\cf5 ]):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     maybe_a_duck\cf5 .\cf13 quack\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This syntax may look a little unfamiliar if you haven't dealt with Mojo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul parameters}} before. What the first signature means is that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 maybe_a_duck
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is an argument of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DuckType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , where 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DuckType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a type that must conform to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Quackable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Quackable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is called the type bound for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DuckType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Some[Quackable]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  form expresses the same idea: the type of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 maybe_a_duck
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is some concrete type that conforms to the trait 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Quackable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
Both forms work the same, except that the first form explicitly names the type value. This can be useful, for example, if you want to take two values of the same type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  take_two_quackers\cf5 [\cf13 DuckType\cf5 :\cf13  Quackable\cf5 ](\cf13 quacker1\cf5 :\cf13  DuckType\cf5 ,\cf13  quacker2\cf5 :\cf13  DuckType\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 pass\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Putting it all together\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Using the function is simple enough:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 make_it_quack\cf5 (\cf13 Duck\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15 make_it_quack\cf5 (\cf13 StealthCow\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 Quack\cb1 \
\cb15 Moo!\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Note that you don't need the square brackets when you call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 make_it_quack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : the compiler infers the type of the argument, and ensures the type has the required trait.\
One limitation of traits is that you can't add traits to existing types. For example, if you define a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Numeric
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait, you can't add it to the standard library 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  types. However, the standard library already includes quite a few traits, and we'll be adding more over time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Traits can require static methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In addition to regular instance methods, traits can specify required static methods.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  HasStaticMethod\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 @staticmethod\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 do_stuff\cf5 ():\cf13  \cf5 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  fun_with_traits\cf5 [\cf16 type\cf5 :\cf13  HasStaticMethod\cf5 ]():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf16 type\cf5 .\cf13 do_stuff\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Default method implementations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Often, some or all of the structs that conform to a given trait can use the same implementation for a given required method. In this case, the trait can include a default implementation. A conforming struct can still define its own version of the method, overriding the default implementation. But if the struct doesn't define its own version, it automatically inherits the default implementation.\
Defining a default implementation for a trait looks the same as writing a method for a struct:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  DefaultQuackable\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Quack"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 DefaultDuck\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  DefaultQuackable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 pass\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When looking at the API doc for a standard library trait, you'll see methods that you must implement listed as required methods, and methods that have default implementations listed as provided methods.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/equality_comparable/Equatable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Equatable}} trait is a good example of the use case for default implementations. The trait includes two methods: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __eq__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (corresponding to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ==
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator) and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __ne__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (corresponding to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 !=
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator). Every type that conforms to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Equatable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  needs to define the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __eq__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method for itself, but the trait supplies a default implementation for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __ne__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Given an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __eq__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method, the definition of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __ne__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is trivial for most types:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  \cf16 __ne__\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  other\cf5 :\cf13  Self\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Bool\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 return\cf13  \cf5 not\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 __eq__\cf5 (\cf13 other\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Trait compositions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can compose traits using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 &
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  sigil. This lets you define new traits that are simple combinations of other traits. You can use a trait composition anywhere that you'd use a single trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  Flyable\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 fly\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13  \cf5 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  quack_and_go\cf5 [\cf16 type\cf5 :\cf13  Quackable \cf5 &\cf13  Flyable\cf5 ](\cf13 quacker\cf5 :\cf13  \cf16 type\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     quacker\cf5 .\cf13 quack\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     quacker\cf5 .\cf13 fly\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 FlyingDuck\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Quackable\cf5 ,\cf13  Flyable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 quack\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Quack"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 fly\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Whoosh!"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword to create a shorthand name for a trait composition:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf13  DuckLike \cf5 =\cf13  Quackable \cf5 &\cf13  Flyable\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 ToyDuck\cf5 (\cf13 DuckLike\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # ... implementation omitted\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also compose traits using \cf5 \ul refinement\cf2 \ulnone , by defining a new, empty trait like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  DuckTrait\cf5 (\cf13 Quackable\cf5 ,\cf13  Flyable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 pass\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However, this is less flexible than using a trait composition and not recommended. The difference is that using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 trait
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword defines a new, named trait. For a struct to conform to this trait, you need to explicitly include it in the struct's signature. On the other hand, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DuckLike
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value represents a composition of two separate traits, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Quackable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Flyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and anything that conforms to those two traits conforms to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DuckLike
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For example, consider the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 FlyingDuck
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type shown above:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 FlyingDuck\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Quackable\cf5 ,\cf13  Flyable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # ... etc\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 FlyingDuck
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conforms to both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Quackable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Flyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , it also conforms to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DuckLike
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait composition. But it doesn't conform to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DuckTrait
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , since it doesn't include 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DuckTrait
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in its list of traits.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Trait refinement\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Traits refine other traits. A trait that refines another trait includes all of the requirements declared by the original trait. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  Animal\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 make_sound\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb15 # Bird refines Animal\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  Bird\cf5 (\cf13 Animal\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 fly\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Since 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bird
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  refines 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Animal
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , a struct that conforms to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bird
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait must implement both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 make_sound()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fly()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . And since every 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bird
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conforms to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Animal
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , a struct that conforms to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bird
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can be passed to any function that requires an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Animal
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
You can define a trait as a refinement of multiple traits by listing them in parentheses. This can be either a comma-separated list of traits or a trait composition. For example, you might define a NamedAnimal trait that combines the requirements of the Animal trait and a new Named trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  Named\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 get_name\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  String\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  NamedAnimal\cf5 (\cf13 Animal\cf5 ,\cf13  Named\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 emit_name_and_sound\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Refinement is useful when you're creating a new trait that adds additional requirements. If you simply want to express the union of two or more traits, use a simple trait composition instead:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf13  NamedAnimal \cf5 =\cf13  Animal \cf5 &\cf13  Named\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Traits and lifecycle methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Traits can specify required {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/#lifecycles-and-lifetimes"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul lifecycle methods}}, including constructors, copy constructors and move constructors.\
For example, the following code creates a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MassProducible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait. A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MassProducible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type has a default (no-argument) constructor and can be moved. It uses two built-in traits: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Defaultable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Defaultable}}, which requires a default (no-argument) constructor, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Movable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Movable}}, which requires the type to have a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#move-constructor"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul move constructor}}.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 factory[]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function returns a newly-constructed instance of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MassProducible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type. The following example shows the definitions of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Defaultable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  traits in comments for reference:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb15 # trait Defaultable\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb15 #     def __init__(out self): ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf6 \cb15 # trait Movable\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb15 #     def __init__(out self, *, deinit take: Self): ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf13  MassProducible \cf5 =\cf13  Defaultable \cf5 &\cf13  Movable\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  factory\cf5 [\cf16 type\cf5 :\cf13  MassProducible\cf5 ]()\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  \cf16 type\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 return\cf13  \cf16 type\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 Thing\cf5 (\cf13 MassProducible\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  \cf16 id\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 __init__\cf5 (out\cf13  self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 id\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf20 0\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 __init__\cf5 (out\cf13  self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 *,\cf13  \cf5 deinit\cf13  take\cf5 :\cf13  Self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 id\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  take\cf5 .\cf16 id\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 var\cf13  thing \cf5 =\cf13  factory\cf5 [\cf13 Thing\cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Register passable types and the RegisterPassable trait\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 "Register passable" tells Mojo that a type should be passed in machine registers such as a CPU register. This means the type is always passed by value. For data types like an integer or floating-point number, this is much more efficient than storing values in stack memory.\
Mojo supports two forms of register passable types.\
The standard register passable type has the following capabilities and restrictions:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
They have normal lifecycles and can implement or override lifecycle methods.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Every field must either be register passable or trivially register passable.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The type must be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#move-constructor"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Movable}}.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Their self pointer is neither stable nor predictable. They can move in memory at any time so they aren't suitable for types that rely on pointer identity.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When used as arguments or results, they can be exposed directly to C and C++ through foreign function interfaces (FFI) and don't need to be passed by pointer.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
They can't override the default move constructor. This guarantees moves are always side-effect-free.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can create custom register-passable types by conforming a type to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 RegisterPassable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait\
Trivial register types are typically data types. They include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Arithmetic types. This includes types such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  etc.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Pointers. The address value is trivial, not the data being pointed to.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Arrays of other trivial types. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a good example.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 These types are provided by Mojo's standard library.\
A trivially register passable type has the following capabilities and restrictions:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
They do not use lifecycle methods.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Every field must be trivially register passable.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The type must be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#copy-constructor"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Copyable}}.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
They use a special trivial (no-op) destructor. They are trivially movable (via 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which refines 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), trivially copyable, and trivially destructible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Built-in traits\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo standard library includes many traits. They're implemented by a number of standard library types, and you can also implement these on your own types. These standard library traits include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/math/Absable/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Absable}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/anytype/AnyType/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul AnyType}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/bool/Boolable/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Boolable}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
