\cf2 The API reference docs linked above include usage examples for each trait. The following sections discuss a few of these traits.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 The 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 Sized
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  trait\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/len/Sized/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Sized}} trait identifies types that have a measurable length, like strings and arrays.\
Specifically, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Sized
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  requires a type to implement the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __len__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method. This trait is used by the built-in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/len/len/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul len()}} function. For example, if you're writing a custom list type, you could implement this trait so your type works with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 len()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 MyList\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Sized\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  size\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 __init__\cf5 (out\cf13  self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf13 size \cf5 =\cf13  \cf20 0\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 __len__\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 size\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 len\cf5 (\cf13 MyList\cf5 ()))\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 The 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 Intable
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 IntableRaising
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  traits\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/Intable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Intable}} trait identifies a type that can be converted to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/IntableRaising/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul IntableRaising}} trait describes a type can be converted to an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but the conversion might raise an error.\
Both of these traits require the type to implement the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __int__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 IntLike\cf5 (\cf13 Intable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  i\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 __int__\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Int\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf13 i\cb1 \
\
\cb15 value \cf5 =\cf13  IntLike\cf5 (\cf20 42\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf13 Int\cf5 (\cf13 value\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf20 42\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 True\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 The 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  trait\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Writable}} trait describes a type that can produce a human-readable text representation by writing to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Writer}} object. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/io/print/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul print()}} function requires that its arguments conform to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait. This enables efficient stream-based writing by default, avoiding unnecessary intermediate 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  heap allocations.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait requires a type to implement a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/#write_to"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul write_to()}} method. This method receives a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut writer: Some[Writer]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument. A generic {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Writer}} that represents anything that can be written to, such as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  buffer, a file, or a network stream. You invoke the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writer/#write"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul write()}} method to write a sequence of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments constituting the string representation of your type.\
An important feature of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait is that it only accepts valid UTF-8 text. This means that when you write data to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can only use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringSlice
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values (via the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writer/#write_string"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul write_string()}} method) or other 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  types; you can't write arbitrary bytes. This guarantee ensures that types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can safely implement the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait without risking corruption of their internal UTF-8 data.\

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  also provides a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/#write_repr_to"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul write_repr_to()}} method for producing the "official" string representation of a type\'97the kind you would see when calling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/repr/repr/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul repr()}} or using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \{!r\}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  format specifier. If at all possible, this should look like a valid Mojo expression that could be used to recreate a struct instance with the same value. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 write_repr_to()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has a default implementation that uses reflection, so you only need to override it if your type needs a distinct debug representation.\
Here is a simple example of a type that implements 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with a custom 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 write_repr_to()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 Dog\cf5 (\cf13 Copyable\cf5 ,\cf13  Writable\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  name\cf5 :\cf13  String\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  age\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf6 # Allows the type to be written into any `Writer`\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 write_to\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 mut\cf13  writer\cf5 :\cf13  Some\cf5 [\cf13 Writer\cf5 ]):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         t\cf17 "Dog(\{self.name\}, \{self.age\})"\cf5 .\cf13 write_to\cf5 (\cf13 writer\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf6 # Official representation when calling `repr`\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 write_repr_to\cf5 (\cf13 self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 mut\cf13  writer\cf5 :\cf13  Some\cf5 [\cf13 Writer\cf5 ]):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         t\cf17 "Dog(name=\{self.name\}, age=\{self.age\})"\cf5 .\cf13 write_to\cf5 (\cf13 writer\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 dog \cf5 =\cf13  Dog\cf5 (\cf17 "Rex"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf20 5\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf16 repr\cf5 (\cf13 dog\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf13 dog\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 Dog(name=Rex, age=5)\cb1 \
\cb15 Dog(Rex, 5)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Lifetime management traits\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo provides two core traits for managing value lifetimes:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/anytype/AnyType/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul AnyType}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
: The base trait that all types extend. Types conforming only to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  may require explicit destruction.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/anytype/ImplicitlyDestructible/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul ImplicitlyDestructible}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
: Types that can be automatically destroyed by calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when their lifetime ends.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For detailed information about value destruction, explicit destruction with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @explicit_destroy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator, and when to use each approach, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Death of a value}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Generic structs with traits\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also use traits when defining a generic container. A generic container is a container (for example, an array or hashmap) that can hold different data types. In a dynamic language like Python it's easy to add different types of items to a container. But in a statically-typed environment, the compiler needs to be able to identify the types at compile time. For example, if the container needs to copy a value, the compiler needs to verify that the type can be copied.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/list/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul List}} type is an example of a generic container. A single 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can only hold a single type of data. The list elements must conform to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 List\cf5 [\cf13 T\cf5 :\cf13  Copyable\cf5 ]:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For example, you can create a list of integer values like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 var\cf13  \cf16 list\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf16 \cb15 list\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf20 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf20 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf20 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf20 4\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf16 range\cf5 (\cf16 len\cf5 (\cf16 list\cf5 )):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf16 list\cf5 [\cf13 i\cf5 ],\cf13  end\cf5 =\cf17 " "\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 1 2 3 4\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can use traits to define requirements for elements that are stored in a container. For example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  requires elements that can be moved and copied. To store a struct in a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the struct needs to conform to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait, which requires a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#copy-constructor"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul copy constructor}} and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#move-constructor"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul move constructor}}.\
Building generic containers is an advanced topic. For an introduction, see the section on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#parameterized-structs"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul parameterized structs}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  members for generics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In addition to methods, a trait can include 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members, which must be defined by any conforming struct. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  Repeater\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 comptime\cf13  count\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 An implementing struct must define a concrete constant value for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  member, using any compile-time parameter value. For example, it can use a literal constant or a compile-time expression, including one that uses the struct's parameters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 Doublespeak\cf5 (\cf13 Repeater\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 comptime\cf13  count\cf5 :\cf13  Int \cf5 =\cf13  \cf20 2\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 Multispeak\cf5 [\cf13 verbosity\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ](\cf13 Repeater\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 comptime\cf13  count\cf5 :\cf13  Int \cf5 =\cf13  Self\cf5 .\cf13 verbosity \cf5 *\cf13  \cf20 2\cf13  \cf5 +\cf13  \cf20 1\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Doublespeak
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct has a constant value for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 count
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Multispeak
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct lets the user set the value using a parameter:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 repeater \cf5 =\cf13  Multispeak\cf5 [\cf20 12\cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that the field is named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 count
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Multispeak
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter is named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 verbosity
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Parameters and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members are in the same namespace, so the parameter can't have the same name as the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  member.\

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members are most useful for writing traits for generic types. For example, imagine that you want to write a trait that describes a generic stack data structure that stores elements that conform to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait.\
By adding the element type as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  member on the trait, you can specify generic methods on the trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 trait\cf13  Stacklike\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 comptime\cf13  EltType\cf5 :\cf13  Copyable\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 push\cf5 (mut\cf13  self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 var\cf13  item\cf5 :\cf13  Self\cf5 .\cf13 EltType\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 pop\cf5 (mut\cf13  self\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Self\cf5 .\cf13 EltType\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The following struct implements the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Stacklike
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait using a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as the underlying storage:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 struct\cf13  \cf16 MyStack\cf5 [\cf16 type\cf5 :\cf13  Copyable \cf5 &\cf13  ImplicitlyDestructible\cf5 ](\cf13 Stacklike\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf17 """A simple Stack built using a List."""\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 comptime\cf13  EltType \cf5 =\cf13  Self\cf5 .\cf16 type\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 comptime\cf13  list_type \cf5 =\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Self\cf5 .\cf13 EltType\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf13  \cf16 list\cf5 :\cf13  Self\cf5 .\cf13 list_type\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 __init__\cf5 (out\cf13  self\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 list\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  Self\cf5 .\cf13 list_type\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 push\cf5 (mut\cf13  self\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 var\cf13  item\cf5 :\cf13  Self\cf5 .\cf13 EltType\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         self\cf5 .\cf16 list\cf5 .\cf13 append\cf5 (\cf13 item\cf5 ^)\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  \cf16 pop\cf5 (mut\cf13  self\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Self\cf5 .\cf13 EltType\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf16 list\cf5 .\cf13 pop\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 def\cf13  dump\cf5 [\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         WritableEltType\cf5 :\cf13  Writable \cf5 &\cf13  Copyable\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 ](\cf13 self\cf5 :\cf13  MyStack\cf5 [\cf13 WritableEltType\cf5 ]):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "["\cf5 ,\cf13  end\cf5 =\cf17 ""\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 for\cf13  item \cf5 in\cf13  self\cf5 .\cf16 list\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15             \cf5 print(\cf13 item\cf5 ,\cf13  end\cf5 =\cf17 ", "\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "]"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyStack
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type adds a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 dump()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that prints the contents of the stack. Because a struct that conforms to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is not necessarily printable, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyStack
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  uses conditional conformance to define a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 dump()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that works as long as the element type is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul writable}}.\
The following code exercises this new trait by defining a generic method, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 add_to_stack()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that adds an item to any 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Stacklike
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  add_to_stack\cf5 [\cf13 S\cf5 :\cf13  Stacklike\cf5 ](mut\cf13  stack\cf5 :\cf13  S\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf5 var\cf13  item\cf5 :\cf13  S\cf5 .\cf13 EltType\cf5 )\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     stack\cf5 .\cf13 push\cf5 (\cf13 item\cf5 ^)\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  \cf16 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     s \cf5 =\cf13  MyStack\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     add_to_stack\cf5 (\cf13 s\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf20 12\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     add_to_stack\cf5 (\cf13 s\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf20 33\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     s\cf5 .\cf13 dump\cf5 ()\cf13              \cf6 # [12, 33, ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 print(\cf13 s\cf5 .\cf13 pop\cf5 ())\cf13        \cf6 # 33\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

